WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.190 --> 00:00:04.390
The B-2 Stealth Bomber is probably one of
the most iconic planes to have ever been built.

00:00:04.390 --> 00:00:09.719
It’s sleek flying wing design makes it practically
invisible to radar and incredibly fuel efficient.

00:00:09.719 --> 00:00:14.389
Allowing it to penetrate even the most sophisticated
of enemy defenses, but how did this incredible

00:00:14.389 --> 00:00:18.820
aircraft come into existence and what technological
advancements made it possible?

00:00:18.820 --> 00:00:22.980
The rise of stealth aircraft can be tracked
back to World War One, when the Germans attempted

00:00:22.980 --> 00:00:27.710
to use transparent canvas to make their planes
difficult to spot. That plan backfired, as

00:00:27.710 --> 00:00:31.410
the glossy canvas made the aircraft even more
visible in sunlight.

00:00:31.410 --> 00:00:35.699
The demand for stealth aircraft, as we think
of them today, rose with the advancement of

00:00:35.699 --> 00:00:41.079
practical radar technology in the late 1930s.
The technology formed the backbone of Britain’s

00:00:41.079 --> 00:00:45.309
early warning system during the battle of
Britain, saving uncountable lives from German

00:00:45.309 --> 00:00:48.670
bombing runs. Let’s see how the technology
works.

00:00:48.670 --> 00:00:54.179
Radar works be sending short pulses of electromagnetic
energy in the form of radio waves outwards,

00:00:54.179 --> 00:00:58.039
the antenna then switches to receiver mode
and waits to detect the reflections of these

00:00:58.039 --> 00:01:02.489
radio waves off distant objects. The radar
now receives a blip on the screen called a

00:01:02.489 --> 00:01:07.330
radar cross-section and it’s size changes
with the magnitude of radio waves returning

00:01:07.330 --> 00:01:10.390
to the antenna.
The Radar Cross-Section is a measure of how

00:01:10.390 --> 00:01:16.990
detectable an object is with radar. The size of the object is just one factor and can be mitigated with clever engineering.

00:01:16.990 --> 00:01:25.720
The B-2 has a 52 m wingspan, yet it has been reported to have the same radar cross-section as a
large bird. So how does the B-2 manage to

00:01:25.720 --> 00:01:30.670
achieve this incredible feat?
The core concept behind the B-2 is reflection.

00:01:30.670 --> 00:01:34.770
It was designed to reflect the radio waves
away from the source, so that they never get

00:01:34.770 --> 00:01:39.950
the chance to be detected. What is amazing
is that every surface of the B-2 has been

00:01:39.950 --> 00:01:44.550
designed with this in mind. The aircraft was
designed with the aid of computational models

00:01:44.550 --> 00:01:49.730
and a supercomputer, which resulted in an
incredibly complex curved shape. This technology

00:01:49.730 --> 00:01:54.560
was not available during the development of
the F-117 Nighthawk, resulting in its much

00:01:54.560 --> 00:01:59.890
simpler faceted flat panels.
The B-2s radar cross section is further reduced

00:01:59.890 --> 00:02:04.350
by its streamlined flying wing design, with
its highly reflective engines embedded within

00:02:04.350 --> 00:02:09.170
the aircraft where radar cannot see them.
Even the engines air intakes and exhaust vents

00:02:09.170 --> 00:02:13.390
are located on top of the plane to ensure
they cannot be detected by ground based devices.

00:02:13.390 --> 00:02:17.980
But the flying wing has some unique flying
characteristics that took many years for Northtrop

00:02:17.980 --> 00:02:22.880
to perfect. One of the most notable is the
lack of a tail rudder to control yaw. The

00:02:22.880 --> 00:02:27.880
B-2 instead uses a split rudders, on the tips
of the left and right wing. They act as airbrakes

00:02:27.880 --> 00:02:32.840
to slow either side of the wing and cause
a yawing motion. But, when in use, split rudders

00:02:32.840 --> 00:02:37.540
can increase the radar cross section of the
plane and so the B-2 can also use differential

00:02:37.540 --> 00:02:42.310
thrusting of its left and right engines to
allow it be controlled when stealth is a priority.

00:02:42.310 --> 00:02:47.590
Beyond its shape the B-2 is also made with
advanced composite materials capable of absorbing

00:02:47.590 --> 00:02:52.450
and dissipating incoming radio energy. The
exact composition of the B-2 is classified,

00:02:52.450 --> 00:02:57.300
but we know that the skin is made from a carbon
fiber reinforced plastic, while the leading

00:02:57.300 --> 00:03:02.150
edge of the B-2 is likely painted with a paint
which contains small particles of iron, which

00:03:02.150 --> 00:03:05.610
absorb electromagnetic energy and converts
it to heat.

00:03:05.610 --> 00:03:10.610
With these technologies combined the B-2 barely
even registers on radar screens. What is even

00:03:10.610 --> 00:03:15.160
more terrifying to think about is the fact
the Nazi ’s had created a very similar plane

00:03:15.160 --> 00:03:21.920
all the way back in 1944. The Horten 229 incorporated
many of the same principles as the B-2, long

00:03:21.920 --> 00:03:26.709
before the stealth technology that made the
B-2 possible was fully understood. Today,

00:03:26.709 --> 00:03:30.720
we can only imagine what the impact this plane
would have had, if it was ready before the

00:03:30.720 --> 00:03:57.430
wars end.

